    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the specificity requirements for certain dermatological presentations in our recent audits. Specifically, when we see atypical morphology—like irregular, pigmented lesions that don't quite fit the standard melanocytic nevus criteria, or dermatological manifestations with unusual, granular, or uneven textures—the claims are being flagged for insufficient specificity. Can you clarify the hierarchy for selecting ICD-10 codes when the skin irregularity presents with these non-standard, irregular, or \"unusual\" structural characteristics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When unusual dermatological presentations lack a precise fit within specific morphology codes, prioritize codes that describe the *nature* of the lesion rather than just its appearance. Focus on codes related to \"other specified neoplasms of skin\" or \"pigmentary disorders\" if atypical pigmentation is the main feature. If the texture is the most prominent unusual characteristic, codes referencing specific skin conditions exhibiting those textural changes, such as seborrheic keratosis variations, may offer better specificity than a general \"skin lesion\" code. Always review the accompanying clinical documentation to ensure the chosen code accurately reflects the predominant clinical feature being audited.",
      "source": "target",
