    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm getting specific pushback on cases where the lesions have a very \"gnarled\" or \"bumpy\" appearance that seems more structural than just pigmented. For example, we have a few cases of subepidermal blisters and granular, uneven textures that look almost... well, they lack a smooth, clean dermatological profile. Is there a specific way to code for these highly irregular, jagged-looking textural anomalies so they don't get lumped into a generic category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
